{
  "image_name": "0d1cff5c806ce0377dccff117c31d10d.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a strategy for engineering CAR T cells to better target heterogeneous glioblastoma by combining logical recognition rules. A standard tandem CAR at the left is shown as recognizing antigen A or B directly, which broadens targeting but can reduce specificity. The main concept instead splits recognition into two steps: a highly specific “priming” signal and a broader “killing” signal. In the central schematic, a synNotch receptor first detects a tumour-specific but heterogeneous neo-epitope, EGFRvIII, which then induces expression of a CAR that can attack cells carrying either IL13Ra2 or EphA2. This creates an AND gate layered onto an OR gate: the T cell acts only where antigen A is present, then kills cells with B or C nearby.\n\nThe right-side diagrams emphasize the tradeoff this solves: A alone offers high specificity but limited coverage, while B or C alone gives more comprehensive killing but less specificity. Their combination yields composite recognition with both benefits. The lower part shows the biological consequence: T cells become primed only inside the tumour, kill neighboring primed and non-primed cancer cells within a local region, and then leave the tumour in an unprimed state, reducing off-tumour killing elsewhere.",
